@page "/blogs/enforce-database-schema-standards-automatically-with-ef-core-conventions"
@using BaseComponents
@inherits BasePage

<Content FileName=@nameof(EFCoreSchemaConventions)>
    <ContentBody>
        <What>
            <p>
                <Highlight>EF Core conventions</Highlight> apply model configuration while EF Core builds a model. This article uses conventions to set table names, default string lengths, foreign-key delete behavior, and enum storage.
            </p>
            <p>
                Conventions complement per-entity <Highlight>Fluent API</Highlight> configuration. A convention supplies defaults; explicit configuration can take precedence when a specific entity or relationship needs a different mapping.
            </p>
        </What>

        <Why>
            <p>
                Model defaults can vary by entity, provider, or relationship. For example, SQL Server maps an unconstrained string to <Highlight>nvarchar(max)</Highlight>, enums are stored as numbers by default, and EF Core applies different delete behaviors to required and optional relationships.
            </p>
            <p>
                Without documented rules, developers may configure similar entities differently. Conventions make shared defaults visible in code, while still allowing explicit exceptions where the domain requires them.
            </p>
        </Why>

        <How>
            <h4>How a convention plugs into model building</h4>
            <p>
                Most schema-wide rules implement <Highlight>IModelFinalizingConvention</Highlight>, which EF Core calls once the model is fully defined but before it's frozen. You register conventions in <Highlight>ConfigureConventions</Highlight>, and because that method wants a factory delegate rather than an instance, you typically loop over an injected collection and hand back a delegate for each one:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
public class OrderContext(
    DbContextOptions&lt;OrderContext&gt; options,
    IEnumerable&lt;IConvention&gt; conventions) : DbContext(options)
{
    protected override void ConfigureConventions(ModelConfigurationBuilder configurationBuilder)
    {
        foreach (var convention in conventions)
        {
            configurationBuilder.Conventions.Add(_ =&gt; convention);
        }
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                You can also remove a built-in convention when you intend to replace its behavior. Review the model afterward because removing one default may affect mappings beyond the rule you are changing.
            </p>

            <h4>Standardize table names and schema</h4>
            <p>
                For a model of standalone entity types, this convention assigns lowercase table names in the <Highlight>ordering</Highlight> schema when convention configuration has precedence. It does not account for owned types, table splitting, or inheritance mappings; add model-specific guards and test the finalized model before applying it to those shapes. Explicit mapping can still define exceptions:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
public class TableNamingConvention : IModelFinalizingConvention
{
    private const string Schema = "ordering";

    public void ProcessModelFinalizing(IConventionModelBuilder modelBuilder, IConventionContext&lt;IConventionModelBuilder&gt; context)
    {
        foreach (var entityType in modelBuilder.Metadata.GetEntityTypes())
        {
            if (entityType.Builder.CanSetTable(entityType.Name))
            {
                var tableName = entityType.GetTableName() ?? entityType.Name;
                var name = tableName.ToLowerInvariant();
                entityType.Builder.ToTable(name, Schema);
            }
        }
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>

            <h4>Cap default string lengths</h4>
            <p>
                Any string property without an explicit <Highlight>HasMaxLength</Highlight> falls back to a sensible default instead of an unbounded column:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
public class DefaultStringLengthConvention : IModelFinalizingConvention
{
    private const int DefaultMaxLength = 200;

    public void ProcessModelFinalizing(IConventionModelBuilder modelBuilder, IConventionContext&lt;IConventionModelBuilder&gt; context)
    {
        foreach (var entityType in modelBuilder.Metadata.GetEntityTypes())
        {
            foreach (var property in entityType.GetProperties())
            {
                if (property.ClrType == typeof(string) &amp;&amp; property.GetMaxLength() is null)
                {
                    property.SetMaxLength(DefaultMaxLength);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>

            <h4>Set a conservative default delete behavior</h4>
            <p>
                A global cascade-delete convention can remove dependent data when a principal is deleted. This example uses the more conservative <Highlight>NoAction</Highlight> default; configure cascades explicitly for relationships whose dependent rows should share the principal's lifecycle.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
public class DeleteBehaviorConvention : IForeignKeyAddedConvention
{
    public void ProcessForeignKeyAdded(
        IConventionForeignKeyBuilder foreignKeyBuilder,
        IConventionContext&lt;IConventionForeignKeyBuilder&gt; context)
    {
        foreignKeyBuilder.OnDelete(DeleteBehavior.NoAction);
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Review provider behavior and existing relationships before changing this default. Individual relationships can specify another behavior through their Fluent configuration.
            </p>

            <h4>Store enums as readable strings, not raw integers</h4>
            <p>
                A string conversion stores enum names instead of numeric values. Renaming an enum member can therefore require a data migration:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
public class EnumAsStringConvention : IModelFinalizingConvention
{
    public void ProcessModelFinalizing(IConventionModelBuilder modelBuilder, IConventionContext&lt;IConventionModelBuilder&gt; context)
    {
        foreach (var entityType in modelBuilder.Metadata.GetEntityTypes())
        {
            foreach (var property in entityType.GetProperties())
            {
                var underlyingType = Nullable.GetUnderlyingType(property.ClrType) ?? property.ClrType;
                if (!underlyingType.IsEnum)
                {
                    continue;
                }

                // Instead of 1 for OrderStatus.Processing, it will serialize it as "Processing".
                property.Builder.HasConversion(typeof(string));
                property.Builder.HasMaxLength(64);
            }
        }
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>

            <h4>Register the conventions and generate a migration</h4>
            <p>
                Register each convention the same way you registered interceptors, and add them all as scoped services alongside your context:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;IConvention, TableNamingConvention&gt;();
builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;IConvention, DefaultStringLengthConvention&gt;();
builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;IConvention, DeleteBehaviorConvention&gt;();
builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;IConvention, EnumAsStringConvention&gt;();
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Because these conventions change how the model is built, EF Core will report pending model changes the next time you run <Highlight>dotnet ef migrations has-pending-model-changes</Highlight>, even though you didn't touch a single entity class. Generate a migration, inspect its operations and SQL for unintended mapping or data changes, and apply it only after review:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-bash">
dotnet ef migrations add ApplySchemaConventions
dotnet ef database update
            </CodeSnippet>
        </How>

        <Summary>
            <p>
                Key takeaways from this article:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><Highlight>Conventions</Highlight> implement <Highlight>IConvention</Highlight> and run automatically while EF Core builds the model, so schema standards apply to every entity without manual Fluent configuration</li>
                <li><Highlight>IModelFinalizingConvention</Highlight> can apply schema-wide rules such as table naming, string length defaults, and enum storage</li>
                <li>A dedicated convention interface like <Highlight>IForeignKeyAddedConvention</Highlight> lets you set relationship defaults; use cascade delete selectively to avoid unintended dependent-data deletion</li>
                <li>You can remove or replace any of EF Core's built-in conventions the same way you add your own</li>
                <li>Convention changes affect the model, so they need a migration just like any other schema change</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Explore the <NavLink href="channels/efcore/">EF Core tutorial and learning path</NavLink> for the full Entity Framework Core series. What to read next: pair these schema-wide guardrails with <NavLink href="blogs/add-cross-cutting-concerns-to-ef-core-without-touching-your-repositories/">EF Core interceptors for auditing and connection diagnostics</NavLink>, or go a level deeper with <NavLink href="blogs/catch-slow-queries-and-rewrite-them-on-the-fly-with-ef-core-command-interceptors/">EF Core command interceptors for query performance</NavLink> to see how generated SQL can be inspected and rewritten at runtime.
            </p>
        </Summary>
    </ContentBody>
</Content>
